  "rules": {
    "ip_rating_scope_v1": "The rugged-smartphone reference identifies the first relevant digit as solid-particle protection and the second as liquid-ingress protection. It describes IP67 as solid level 6 plus liquid level 7 and IP68 as solid level 6 plus liquid level 8, with the immersion depth for level 8 determined by the manufacturer. The code is therefore a pair of bounded classifications, not the word waterproof and not a single universal immersion promise.",
    "model_specific_water_conditions_v1": "The captured iPhone page explicitly names iPhone 11 Pro and iPhone 11 Pro Max, then says both models are IP68 water and dust resistant for 30 minutes at 4 meters and says the warranty does not cover water damage. The Samsung Galaxy S22 page states IP68 up to 1.5 meters for 30 minutes. These different added conditions show why IP68 must be read with the exact model statement. Neither statement establishes permanent protection, arbitrary liquids, sweat exposure, rain at every pressure, damaged seals, or repeated hot and humid cycles.",
    "sweat_non_equivalence_v1": "The perspiration material describes eccrine sweat as watery and brackish and says exertion and heat increase sweating. The sweat-diagnostics material says sweat is mostly water but includes sodium and chloride among its solutes. The corrosion reference says temperature, humidity, and salinity affect galvanic corrosion. These sources explain why runner exposure is not identical to an unspecified immersion test, but they do not test this phone, its seals, or any accessory and cannot prove damage or survival.",
    "protection_listing_matrix_v1": "All four protection pages cover the iPhone 11 Pro Max, but only at seller-listing level. The AICase page advertises IP68, a test over 10 feet for 12 hours, a recommended depth around 10 feet, a maximum around 19.6 feet, no posted review, and that the product is not recommended for daily use. The RUNBACH page advertises compatibility with an iPhone 11 Pro Max wearing a slim case, water and sweat resistance, marathon use, and twelve posted reviews, but gives no matching sweat, rain, temperature, salt, or duration test. The universal diving housing advertises IPX8 and 20 meters, lists the iPhone 11 Pro Max, and has no posted review, but its page is not a runner-cycle test. The ordinary Hitaoyou case advertises a 16-foot drop test and shock, scratch, and bump protection for the iPhone 11 Pro Max; those claims do not establish liquid protection or change the phone's own IP rating.",
    "scoped_ingress_experience_v1": "The community material contains beliefs, deliberate exposure choices, disagreement, and one reported warranty dispute. One author calls an IP68 phone waterproof but is uneasy about rinsing it. Another reports putting an IP68 iPhone 13 mini under a tap; replies recommend against deliberate wetting, make unsupported technical and warranty assertions, or report no issue in another personal use. A third author says moisture was cited when an iPhone 13 Pro Max warranty request was refused while denying water exposure. A case thread mixes a general waterproof belief with personal drop-and-case experiences. Treat all of these as scoped individual statements, not controlled evidence, prevalence, warranty policy, or proof about the iPhone 11 Pro Max combinations.",
    "conditional_ingress_trial_v1": "Prefer reducing exposure before relying on a label: keep the phone away from direct sweat and rain when practical, or shortlist a returnable sealed case or housing whose exact fit, closure, instructions, service terms, and stated conditions are acceptable. If the seller permits it, test only the empty accessory with dry absorbent material first, then inspect the seals and use short, reversible training trials with post-run inspection and drying. A single dry check or short run is only a process observation and never a long-term sweat, rain, salt, heat, humidity, or marathon guarantee.",
    "evidence_bounded_runner_decision_v1": "Do not call the bare phone, ordinary shockproof case, sweatproof armband, IP68 case, or diving housing marathon-proof. The admissible outcome is conditional: select the least-exposure returnable setup that passes exact fit, seller-condition, empty-accessory, short-run, usability, and service checks, while keeping the phone out of exposure when it is essential. If exact instructions, closure condition, return or warranty terms, or direct sweat and repeated-cycle evidence are missing, disclose the gap and defer rather than promising survival."
